{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvyKA1o2BzegKnvCsiQC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PXXzTWVrUx4KYNfaQ9Nexc","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('figure_manifest.json'))\nfor f in d['figures']:\n    f['min_font_size_pt']=f['estimated_min_rendered_font_pt']\njson.dump(d,open('figure_manifest.json','w'),indent=2)\nE\ngrep -n -i -E \"3 of 3|three negative|control episodes|selection-bias|selection bias|trade-off|tradeoff|label-dependent|few calibration|vacuous|at the cost of|three controls|controls\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8062,"cache_read_input_tokens":50176,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8062},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ef69e69-c3be-4874-ab3f-3697f6dedd2d","uuid":"a49e07ed-e1a2-4041-bdd6-71a7d0ca7df9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:19:55.097Z","request_id":"req_011CgvyK9FQSKkmSfUgWFrk9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PXXzTWVrUx4KYNfaQ9Nexc","type":"tool_result","content":"36:Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\n40:Many predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibration set into prediction sets whose marginal coverage is at least $1-\\alpha$ under exchangeability, in finite samples and without distributional assumptions. In practice, however, three questions that matter to a user are not answered by the marginal guarantee: does coverage hold within classes or confidence strata, does it survive a change in the input distribution, and is it achieved with sets that are small enough to be informative? Variants that address one of these questions typically do so at the cost of another \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Kiyani2024LengthOI}, so comparisons that report only average coverage and average set size can hide the trade-off.\n42:This paper takes the evaluation protocol as its first-class object. We adopt a single evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families that scores a set-valued predictor on three axes at once (one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio) and that adds three negative controls whose expected behaviour is fixed before scoring. Within this protocol we ask:\n48:(i) We write down the three-axis protocol in reader-checkable form, including the loss, the slice definitions, the negative controls and the validity conditions, and derive simple properties relevant to interpreting scores (for example, that the full prediction set scores exactly 0.75 and that the one-sided gap metrics do not penalise over-coverage).\n56:\\caption{Evaluation protocol at a glance. Simplex-valued softmax outputs from evaluator-generated synthetic families are split into calibration and test parts. The studied procedure (Mondrian LAC with gated domain-classifier weighting) and four evaluator baselines (LAC, APS, RAPS, class-wise LAC) produce label sets, which are scored on three axes (marginal under-coverage with weight 0.5, worst-slice under-coverage 0.25, set-size ratio 0.25) and checked by three negative controls. Scope is limited to the declared synthetic families.}\n65:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n93:\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n96:Three negative controls are run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be near target and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n116:The procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (large $K$) should shrink worst-slice under-coverage relative to pooled calibration, because it makes the coverage guarantee hold within the evaluator's slices when those coincide with the groups. \\textbf{H2}: a gated, cross-fitted density-ratio correction should reduce marginal under-coverage on shifted cases, while a gate that stays closed leaves exchangeable behaviour and the negative controls untouched. \\textbf{H3}: group-indicator quantile-regression thresholds could further reduce worst-slice gaps when the groups are overlapping. The retained evidence addresses these only partially: H1 is not isolated (no pooled-versus-Mondrian pair of otherwise identical configurations was retained, and baseline identities are unknown); H2 is addressed by the development comparison V1 versus V2 and by the sealed shift-case gap; H3 is untestable from V3 because V3 coincides with V2. The aim of the sealed evaluation is thus the narrower statement of Section~\\ref{sec:results} about the composite score, not a verification of each hypothesis.\n132:\\paragraph{Development data.} V1--V3 are scored with seeds 101--103 on the evaluator's development cases (per the declared reset sequence, three seeds on each of four case designs, plus the three controls); all three configurations produced valid runs and none was unscored. The development score of V2 and V3 tie exactly, and V2 is the configuration recorded by the selection manifest.\n142:No episode failed and all three negative controls passed, and the sealed qualification outcome is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.qualification.outcome]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.controls_passed_fraction]]\n166:Negative controls passed & 3 of 3 (fraction 1.0) \\\\\n203:\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} Within the declared synthetic families and this protocol, the studied procedure has a higher composite score than the best of four standard split-conformal baselines on sealed data, with no failed episode and all negative controls passed. The score margin is the net effect of the evaluator's 0.5/0.25/0.25 weighting; it does not show dominance on each axis, since per-axis baseline values are not retained, and a different weighting could change the ranking. The interval half-width is about 0.011 and the margin is roughly twice the per-seed standard deviation of the procedure's scores (0.019), but baseline per-seed scores are not retained, so a seed-level paired effect size cannot be computed.\n217:The synthetic generator is one parametric family (Gaussian logits with a true-class signal, heteroscedastic signal strength, a prior tilt and temperature), and the predictor's calibration is not varied independently; real softmax outputs may behave differently. For $K\\le10$ the evaluator's slices are classes, which are exactly the Mondrian groups of the studied procedure, so part of its advantage on the slice axis reflects a match between method and metric by design; the class-wise baseline shares this alignment, but its individual score is not retained. The development score was used to select the configuration, so development numbers are optimistic selection-biased proxies; the sealed evaluation was run once on disjoint data. Finally, the composite loss multiplies the marginal gap by $1/\\alpha$ and clips it, so under-coverage beyond $\\alpha$ in a single case is not distinguished; the retained aggregates cannot show whether any case hit this clip.\n221:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For users, the results suggest evaluating conformal sets on compositional outputs with at least a marginal, a slice-level and a size criterion, and with controls that expose vacuous behaviour; reporting only average coverage and size would not have revealed the residual shift and slice gaps seen here. A stronger study built on the same protocol would additionally report per-baseline and per-case scores, a within-case ablation that separates the Mondrian grouping, the gate and the weighting, activation logs for the gate, and at least one evaluation on classifier outputs from a natural dataset. We also recommend reporting the three components separately for every method (Figure~\\ref{fig:decomp}).\n224:We studied a Mondrian least-ambiguous-set conformal procedure with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step under a multi-axis reliability protocol on synthetic softmax families. On sealed final data it reached a composite score of 0.8416 versus 0.8029 for the best of four standard baselines, a paired margin of 0.0387 with a bootstrap 95\\% interval of 0.0274--0.0499, with no failed episodes and all three negative controls passed. The same evaluation shows that the procedure does not remove under-coverage under shift (0.0536) or on worst slices (0.0675), and the loss decomposition identifies marginal under-coverage as the largest residual contributor. The conclusions are conditional on the declared synthetic families, on one aggregated best-baseline comparison, and on evaluator-defined weights. The most useful next steps are per-case and per-baseline reporting, ablations that isolate the gate and the weighting from the Mondrian grouping, gate-activation logging, and an evaluation on real classifier outputs; none of these has been done here.\n267:The search-time evaluator generates data with the logit model of Section~\\ref{sec:setup}; the sealed final plane uses the same scoring, baseline and bootstrap code with different cases and seeds. Slices require at least 30 test points. Controls: C1 permutes calibration labels; C2 permutes test labels when scoring; C3 replaces all probabilities by the uniform vector. A control episode is discarded as vacuous if fewer than half of the permuted labels changed. The coverage tolerance for exchangeable cases is $3\\sqrt{\\alpha(1-\\alpha)(1/(n_{\\mathrm{cal}}+2)+1/n_{\\mathrm{test}})/n_{\\mathrm{seeds}}}$. The final evaluator source file is not separately retained, so statements about the final plane rely on the plane-conditional branches of the shared evaluator and on the qualification outcome.\n273:\\caption{Development-time metrics for the three nested configurations (seeds 101--103; lower is better for gaps and size ratio, higher for score). Controls passed fraction was 1.0 and failed episodes 0 for all three.}\n289:\\caption{Per-seed scores. Final seeds are sealed-evaluation runs (duration in seconds includes control episodes); development seeds are for V1/V2 (V3 identical to V2).}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ef69e69-c3be-4874-ab3f-3697f6dedd2d","uuid":"a566c65d-e266-441b-a555-2cdb5c5817ed","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:19:55.178Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"36:Softmax outputs are compositions: non-negative vectors summing to one. Conformal prediction turns them into label sets with a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee, but marginal coverage says little about reliability for particular classes, confidence strata, or shifted data. We study a split-conformal procedure that combines Mondrian (class- or confidence-bin-conditional) calibration of the least-ambiguous-set score with a gated, cross-fitted domain-classifier reweighting step that reduces exactly to the unweighted procedure when no shift is detected. We evaluate it under a fixed, evaluator-owned protocol on synthetic softmax families: a composite score combining one-sided marginal under-coverage, worst-slice under-coverage, and set-size ratio, plus three negative controls. On sealed final data (five seeded runs, no failed episodes, all controls passed) the procedure scores 0.8416 against 0.8029 for the best of four standard split-conformal baselines; the paired margin is 0.0387 (bootstrap 95\\% interval 0.0274--0.0499). Residual under-coverage remains on shifted cases (0.0536) and worst slices (0.0675). The result is conditional on the declared synthetic families; baseline identities, per-case results and real-data behaviour are unavailable.\n40:Many predictors return a composition: a vector of non-negative entries summing to one, such as a classifier's softmax probabilities. Users of such outputs need to know when the associated uncertainty statement can be trusted. Conformal prediction converts a score function and a held-out calibratio