:Exact conditional coverage is impossible in general without further structure, which has motivated relaxations that target class-, group- or feature-conditional guarantees \\citep{Gibbs2023ConformalPW,Bairaktari2025KandinskyCP}. Conditional conformal prediction has long been studied as a way to reduce bias in label-dependent coverage \\citep{Löfström2015BiasRT}, and recent work addresses class-wise coverage when classes are many, long-tailed or have few calibration examples \\citep{Ding2023ClassConditionalCP,Ding2025ConformalPF,Shi2024ConformalPF}. Group-conditional thresholds have also been learned by quantile regression \\citep{Melki2023GroupConditionalCP}, trust scores have been used to improve conditional coverage \\citep{Kaur2025ConformalPS}, and coverage conditional on a selection event has been studied \\citep{Jin2024ConfidenceOT}. Our procedure uses the simplest member of this family, Mondrian groups defined by label or by confidence bin, and we examine a quantile-regression variant only as a development-time comparison.\n93:\\subsection{Baselines, negative controls and validity conditions}\n96:Three negative controls are run on one case and seed: (C1) permuted calibration labels, where sets must inflate (size ratio at least $1.5\\times$ the matched case or at least $0.9$); (C2) shuffled test labels, where coverage must not exceed chance by more than a $3\\sigma$ tolerance and must be well below matched coverage or sets near full; (C3) uniform probability inputs, where coverage must be near target and mean set size at least $(1-\\alpha)K-1$. A run is valid only if no episode fails, all controls pass, calibration and test data are content-hash disjoint (and, for the final plane, disjoint from the search data), and exchangeable cases do not under-cover by more than a $3\\sigma$ finite-sample tolerance.\n116:The procedure is built around three expectations, each tied to one component. \\textbf{H1}: grouping calibration by label (small $K$) or confidence bin (l